<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__asprey_2000_contois_kinetics> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) are dynamic models of microbial fed-batch fermentation that track biomass and substrate-related concentrations. Anane 2017 models E. coli glucose use and acetate overflow/cycling, including oxygen transfer, and is fitted to fermentation measurements. Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) models baker’s yeast with a Contois/Canoid-type substrate-consumption relation and is used with simulated data to demonstrate kinetic-model discrimination and experiment design. They concern different organisms and model different kinetic detail and purposes, but are comparable as fed-batch fermentation models." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.658 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__asprey_2000_monod_kinetics> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) are dynamic models of fed-batch microbial fermentation that predict biomass and substrate-related concentrations while accounting for feed operation. Anane 2017 models non-recombinant E. coli and explicitly represents glucose partitioning, acetate cycling, and dissolved-oxygen dynamics, fitted to physical culture data. Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) models baker’s yeast with Monod substrate-consumption kinetics and biomass and substrate balances, and uses simulated data to demonstrate model checking and experiment design. They are comparable as fed-batch fermentation models, but describe different organisms and metabolic detail, and serve different purposes." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.697 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__birol_2002> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Birol 2002" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Birol 2002 are mechanistic models of glucose-fed batch-to-fed-batch microbial fermentations, and both predict biomass, glucose, and dissolved oxygen for process development, optimization, or control. Anane 2017 models E. coli overflow metabolism and continuous acetate production and re-assimilation, including dissolved-oxygen probe response. Birol 2002 models penicillin production, including non-growth-associated product formation and hydrolysis, and adds pH and temperature control, gas transfer, and material and energy balances. Thus, they share the fed-batch fermentation setting and several predicted quantities, but focus on different organisms and products and represent different process phenomena." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.722 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__nausch_2023> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Nausch 2023" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Nausch 2023 are unstructured kinetic models of cell growth and extracellular substrate uptake in stirred bioreactors, with batch cultivation followed by feeding and an aim of supporting process optimization. Anane 2017 models E. coli glucose use and acetate overflow/re-assimilation in fed-batch, including oxygen transfer and probe response; Nausch 2023 models tobacco BY-2 growth on sucrose-derived sugars and mineral nutrients, distinguishing active and dead biomass during semi-continuous feeding and broth draining. They are useful to compare as different kinetic treatments of biomass and nutrient dynamics under fed cultivation, but describe different organisms, substrates, and feeding operations." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.713 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__wang_2019> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Wang 2019" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Wang 2019 are dynamic cultivation models that represent how carbon is partitioned among biomass and other metabolic products, and both predict culture responses under changing conditions. Anane 2017 models glucose use, acetate overflow and re-assimilation in fed-batch E. coli as glucose supply changes. Wang 2019 models carbon fixation and allocation among functional biomass, carbohydrates and lipids in Dunaliella viridis as light and nitrogen conditions change. They address different organisms and mechanisms: Anane 2017 focuses on glucose and acetate cycling, while Wang 2019 focuses on light-dependent photosynthesis and nitrogen-regulated storage." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.7 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__xing_2010> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Xing 2010" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Xing 2010 are dynamic kinetic models of fed-batch bioreactor cultures, predicting cell growth alongside extracellular culture variables and intended to support process development or improvement. Anane 2017 models E. coli glucose use, acetate overflow and re-assimilation, and oxygen dynamics across batch and fed-batch phases; Xing 2010 models CHO-cell growth and B1 production, with glucose, glutamine, ammonia, and lactate dynamics under daily feeding. They therefore address the same broad operation but different organisms and mechanisms, and Xing 2010 additionally models product formation and is validated at 500-L and 5,000-L scales. Neither is described as building on the other." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.756 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__zentou_2019_andrews> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Zentou 2019 (Andrews)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Zentou 2019 (Andrews) are dynamic models of microbial fermentation in bioreactors, both predicting biomass and substrate changes and proposed for process optimization or control. Anane 2017 models fed-batch E. coli growth with glucose overflow and acetate cycling, including dissolved oxygen; Zentou 2019 (Andrews) models batch molasses fermentation by S. cerevisiae for ethanol production. Their focus and kinetics differ: Anane 2017 represents glucose partitioning and acetate production and uptake, while Zentou 2019 (Andrews) uses an Andrews substrate-inhibition growth law and predicts ethanol formation." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.671 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/anane_2017__zentou_2019_monod> rdfs:label "Anane 2017 ~ Zentou 2019 (Monod)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Anane 2017 and Zentou 2019 (Monod) are dynamic bioreactor fermentation models that predict biomass and substrate-related concentrations over time and are proposed to support process optimization and control. Anane 2017 describes fed-batch E. coli glucose metabolism, including acetate overflow and re-assimilation, oxygen transfer, and probe response; Zentou 2019 (Monod) describes batch molasses fermentation by S. cerevisiae, linking Monod growth to sugar consumption and ethanol production through yield coefficients. They therefore address the same broad unit operation but different organisms, products, operating modes, and modeled phenomena. Zentou 2019 (Monod)’s accuracy declines at high substrate concentrations because it omits substrate inhibition, while Anane 2017 represents glucose partitioning and acetate cycling." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.747 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/asprey_2000_contois_kinetics__asprey_2000_monod_kinetics> rdfs:label "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) ~ Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) and Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) are competing dynamic models of baker’s yeast fermentation in a fed-batch reactor, tracking biomass and substrate concentrations with shared reactor balances and adjustable dilution and feed-substrate profiles. They differ in the substrate-consumption rate law: Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) uses Contois/Canoid-type kinetics, while Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) uses Monod kinetics. The paper tests their distinguishability and designs experiments to discriminate between them using simulated data. Both fit the simulated data well, though the paper judges Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) better suited to it." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.918 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/asprey_2000_contois_kinetics__birol_2002> rdfs:label "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) ~ Birol 2002" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) and Birol 2002 are dynamic fermentation models that include biomass and substrate dynamics in fed-batch operation, and both use Contois-type kinetics. Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) models baker’s yeast and compares its Contois/Canoid substrate-consumption alternative with a Monod alternative, using simulated data to demonstrate model discrimination and experiment design. Birol 2002 models glucose-fed penicillin production with a more detailed unstructured description, including Contois growth alongside product formation, oxygen transfer, heat and pH/temperature effects, and process control. They are comparable as fermentation models using Contois kinetics, but differ in organism, scope, and purpose." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.655 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/asprey_2000_contois_kinetics__xing_2010> rdfs:label "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) ~ Xing 2010" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) and Xing 2010 are dynamic models of fed-batch cell cultures that track cell growth and nutrient-related concentrations, so their kinetic and feeding representations are worth comparing. Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) models baker’s yeast and uses Contois/Canoid substrate-consumption kinetics as an alternative to Monod, mainly to demonstrate model discrimination and experiment design. Xing 2010 models CHO cells producing a B1 antibody fusion protein, with Monod-type nutrient-limited growth plus metabolite inhibition, maintenance, and product formation. Xing 2010 therefore covers more culture variables and is calibrated and validated against production data, whereas Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics)’s comparison uses simulated responses." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.691 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/asprey_2000_contois_kinetics__zentou_2019_monod> rdfs:label "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) ~ Zentou 2019 (Monod)" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) and Zentou 2019 (Monod) are yeast-fermentation models that track biomass and substrate, but they use different operating modes and kinetic formulations. Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) is a fed-batch baker’s yeast model whose Contois/Canoid-type alternative is explicitly compared with a Monod model; Zentou 2019 (Monod) is a Monod model for batch molasses fermentation by Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Zentou 2019 (Monod) also predicts ethanol, while Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) focuses on biomass and substrate. Asprey 2000 (Contois kinetics) demonstrates model discrimination using simulated data, whereas Zentou 2019 (Monod) estimates and validates its model against fermentation experiments." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.717 .

<http://example.org/kg/link/asprey_2000_monod_kinetics__birol_2002> rdfs:label "Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) ~ Birol 2002" ;
    mkg:linkDescription "Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) and Birol 2002 are dynamic fermentation models involving biomass and substrate in fed-batch operation, so they are useful to compare as models of related bioreactor processes. Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics) describes baker’s yeast with two concentration states and Monod-type substrate kinetics, and is used to demonstrate model discrimination and experiment design on simulated data. Birol 2002 models glucose-fed penicillin production, extending the state description to include product, oxygen, volume, temperature, heat generation and carbon dioxide, with additional growth, transfer and control mechanisms. Birol 2002 is compared with published fermentation results, whereas Asprey 2000 (Monod kinetics)’s example is based on simulated responses rather than reported physical-reactor measurements." ;
    mkg:provenance "written by gpt-6-luna from the two Sups' descriptions and facts, 2026-10-29 (A/B replaced by the model names by code)" ;
    mkg:similarity 0.695 .

